---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Password generator: strong random passwords and passphrases, made on this page"
  description="Make strong random passwords with your choice of length and characters, or a memorable passphrase. Generated in your browser with proper randomness, never sent anywhere."
>
  <h1>Password generator</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Press the button and the page makes a password out of genuine randomness, the cryptographic
    kind built into your browser, not a shuffled word list. Nothing is sent to a server, so the
    password exists only on your screen and in whatever password manager you put it in.
  </p>
    <figure class="fig fig--utilities"><img src="/img/password.webp" alt="Lines of code on a screen" width="1200" height="514" decoding="async"></figure>
    <nav class="onpage" aria-label="On this page"><p class="onpage-title">On this page</p><ul><li><a href="#how-long-is-long-enough">How long is long enough</a></li><li><a href="#questions-people-ask">Questions people ask</a></li></ul></nav>

  <div class="sheet">
    <p class="result-figure" id="password" style="word-break:break-all;" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p>
      <button class="btn btn--primary" id="gen" type="button">Make a new one</button>
      <button class="btn" id="copy" type="button" style="margin-left:0.5rem;">Copy it</button>
      <span class="result-note" id="copied" style="margin-left:0.5rem;"></span>
    </p>
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div>
        <label for="length">Length (characters)</label>
        <input type="number" id="length" inputmode="numeric" min="8" max="64" step="1" value="16" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label>Characters to use</label>
        <p style="margin:0;">
          <label style="display:block;color:var(--color-ink-2);"><input type="checkbox" id="c-lower" checked /> Lowercase abc</label>
          <label style="display:block;color:var(--color-ink-2);"><input type="checkbox" id="c-upper" checked /> Uppercase ABC</label>
          <label style="display:block;color:var(--color-ink-2);"><input type="checkbox" id="c-digits" checked /> Digits 123</label>
          <label style="display:block;color:var(--color-ink-2);"><input type="checkbox" id="c-symbols" checked /> Symbols !@#</label>
          <label style="display:block;color:var(--color-ink-2);"><input type="checkbox" id="c-plain" /> Leave out lookalikes (0 O 1 l I)</label>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="mode">Style</label>
        <select id="mode">
          <option value="random">Random characters</option>
          <option value="phrase">Passphrase (words)</option>
        </select>
        <p class="result-note" id="strength"></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="how-long-is-long-enough">How long is long enough</h2>
  <p>
    A password’s strength is mostly its length and how unpredictable each character is, a
    figure called entropy, measured in bits. The strength line under the options does that sum
    for your settings: length × log₂ of the number of possible characters. Sixteen characters
    drawn from letters, digits and symbols gives about 100 bits, which is beyond any guessing
    attack that exists. Twelve characters from the same set, about 77 bits, is fine for most
    accounts. What actually gets accounts stolen is reuse: one site leaks, and the same
    password opens your email. A different long password for each account, kept in a password
    manager, is the whole game.
  </p>
  <p>
    The passphrase style joins five ordinary words with dashes. Five words picked from a list
    of about 1,700 comes to roughly 54 bits, and it is far easier to type on a phone or a TV
    remote than random characters, which is why passphrases suit the accounts you log into by
    hand.
  </p>

  <h2 id="questions-people-ask">Questions people ask</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    <dt>Is it safe to make a password on a web page?</dt>
    <dd>
      On this page, yes, and you do not have to take that on trust: the generator runs entirely
      in your browser using crypto.getRandomValues, the same source your browser uses for
      secure connections. Disconnect from the internet and it still works. No password is ever
      transmitted or stored, here or anywhere.
    </dd>
    <dt>Why not just use my pet’s name and a number?</dt>
    <dd>
      Because attackers’ word lists start with names, teams, and dictionary words with the
      number 1 or the year tacked on. A name plus digits is usually under 30 bits of real
      unpredictability, whatever it looks like. Length and randomness are what hold up.
    </dd>
    <dt>What does NIST actually recommend now?</dt>
    <dd>
      The US standards body, in SP 800-63B, tells services to allow at least 64 characters,
      accept spaces, stop forcing regular password changes, and check new passwords against
      lists of known leaked ones. For you the practical version is: long, unique per site, in
      a manager, with two factor authentication on the accounts that matter.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p class="meta">Method: crypto.getRandomValues with rejection sampling, no Math.random, nothing transmitted. Sources: NIST SP 800-63B. Page last checked 13 November 2026.</p>

